e I made a mistake in the formula.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to recall that the tangent line at (x1, y1) on circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + Dx + Ey + F = 0 is:\n\nxx1 + yy1 + D*(x +x1)/2 + E*(y + y1)/2 + F = 0\n\nSo, in our case, F=0, so:\n\nxx1 + yy1 -5*(x +x1)/2 + E*(y + y1)/2 = 0\n\nSo, for point B(5,0):\n\nx*5 + y*0 -5*(x +5)/2 + E*(y +0)/2 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n5x - (5x +25)/2 + (E y)/2 = 0\n\nMultiply all terms by 2:\n\n10x -5x -25 + E y = 0\n\nSo, 5x -25 + E y =0\n\nThus, 5x + E y =25.\n\nSimilarly, for point C(22/5, 12\u221a14/5):\n\nEquation of tangent is x*(22/5) + y*(12\u221a14/5) -5*(x +22/5)/2 + E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2 =0\n\nMultiply through:\n\n(22/5)x + (12\u221a14/5)y -5*(x +22/5)/2 + E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2 =0\n\nSimplify each term:\n\nFirst term: (22/5)x\n\nSecond term: (12\u221a14/5)y\n\nThird term: -5*(x +22/5)/2 = - (5x +22)/2\n\nFourth term: E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2\n\nSo, all together:\n\n(22/5)x + (12\u221a14/5)y - (5x +22)/2 + E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2 =0\n\nWait, maybe better to compute each term step by step.\n\nCompute third term:\n\n-5*(x +22/5)/2 = - (5x +22)/2\n\nSimilarly, E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to use the same formula as for point B.\n\nWait, maybe I should use the standard tangent formula.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to use the point D as the intersection of two tangents.\n\nWe have tangent at B: 5x + Ey =25.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at C is (22/5)x + (12\u221a14/5)y + something.\n\nWait, maybe instead of trying to compute equations, let me use the power of point D.\n\nGiven that D lies outside the circle, and DA intersects the circle at A and P, so by power of point D, we have DB^2 = DC^2 = DP * DA.\n\nWait, but since D is the intersection of tangents at B and C, so DB=DC.\n\nWait, yes, because tangents from a common external point are equal.\n\nSo, DB=DC.\n\nCompute DB: the length from D to B.\n\nBut wait, we don't know D's coordinates yet.\n\nAlternatively, let me think about coordinates.\n\nGiven that A is (0,0), B is (5,0), C is (22/5, 12\u221a14/5).\n\nWait, no, wait, given triangle with sides AB=5, BC=9, AC=10.\n\nWait, if AB=5, BC=9, AC=10, then coordinates can be set as:\n\nLet me place A at (0,0), B at (5,0), and then C somewhere in the plane.\n\nCompute coordinates of C.\n\nUsing distance formula: AC=10, so C is (x,y) with sqrt(x^2 + y^2)=10.\n\nAnd distance from B to C is 9: sqrt( (x-5)^2 + y^2 )=9.\n\nSo, equations:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = 100\n\n(x-5)^2 + y\u00b2 = 81\n\nSubtract second equation from first:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - (x\u00b2 -10x +25 + y\u00b2) = 100 -81\n\nSimplify:\n\n10x -25 =19\n\n10x=44\n\nx=44/10=22/5=4.4\n\nThen, y\u00b2=100 - (22/5)^2=100 - 484/25=2500/25 -484/25=2016/25\n\nThus, y= sqrt(2016/25)=sqrt(2016)/5= sqrt(16*126)/5=4*sqrt(126)/5=4*sqrt(9*14)/5=4*3*sqrt(14)/5=12\u221a14/5.\n\nThus, coordinates are A(0,0), B(5,0), C(22/5,12\u221a14/5).\n\nNow, find point D, the intersection of tangents at B and C.\n\nFirst, find equations of tangents at B and C.\n\nEquation of tangent at B: as before, using the formula for tangent at (x1,y1) on circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + Dx + Ey + F=0.\n\nOur circle is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x + Ey =0, so D=-5, E=E, F=0.\n\nThus, tangent at point (x1,y1) is xx1 + yy1 -5(x +x1)/2 + E(y + y1)/2 =0.\n\nSo, for point B(5,0):\n\nx*5 + y*0 -5*(x +5)/2 + E*(y +0)/2 =0\n\nSimplify:\n\n5x - (5x +25)/2 + (E y)/2=0\n\nMultiply all terms by 2:\n\n10x -5x -25 + E y=0\n\n5x -25 + E y=0\n\nSo, equation is 5x + E y =25.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at C(22/5, 12\u221a14/5):\n\nx*(22/5) + y*(12\u221a14/5) -5*(x +22/5)/2 + E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2=0.\n\nMultiply all terms by 10 to eliminate denominators:\n\n10x*(22/5) +10y*(12\u221a14/5) -5*2(x +22/5) + E*5(y +12\u221a14/5)=0.\n\nWait, maybe better to compute each term:\n\nx*(22/5) + y*(12\u221a14/5) -5*(x +22/5)/2 + E*(y +12\u221a14/5)/2=0\n\nMultiply each term by 2:\n\n(22/5)*2 x + (12\u221a14/5)*2 y -5*(x +22/5) + E*(y +12\u221a14/5)=0\n\nSimplify:\n\n(44/5)x + (24\u221a14/5)y -5x -22 + E y + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(44/5 -5)x + (24\u221a14/5 + E)y + (-22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5)=0\n\nConvert 5x to 25/5 x:\n\n(44/5 -25/5)x + (24\u221a14/5 + E)y + (-22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5)=0\n\nWhich is:\n\n(19/5)x + (24\u221a14/5 + E)y + (-22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5)=0\n\nSo, the equation is (19/5)x + (24\u221a14/5 + E)y + (-22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5)=0.\n\nBut we can write this as:\n\n19x + (24\u221a14 + 5E)y + (-110 + 12\u221a14 E)=0\n\nWait, no, let me see.\n\nWait, the equation is (19/5)x + (24\u221a14/5 + E)y + (-22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5)=0\n\nMultiply all terms by 5:\n\n19x + (24\u221a14 +5E)y + (-110 +12\u221a14 E)=0\n\nSo, equation is 19x + (24\u221a14 +5E)y =110 -12\u221a14 E.\n\nBut this seems complicated because we don't know E.\n\nWait, but in our circle equation, E is the coefficient of y.\n\nFrom the circle equation, we have E = from the coordinates of C.\n\nWait, the circle passes through A(0,0), B(5,0), and C(22/5, 12\u221a14/5).\n\nSo, plugging A into circle equation: 0 +0 -5*0 + E*0=0, which is 0=0, no info.\n\nPlugging B(5,0): 25 +0 -25 + E*0=0, so 0=0.\n\nPlugging C(22/5, 12\u221a14/5):\n\n(22/5)^2 + (12\u221a14/5)^2 -5*(22/5) + E*(12\u221a14/5)=0.\n\nWait, the circle equation is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x + Ey=0.\n\nSo, plug C(22/5,12\u221a14/5):\n\n(22/5)^2 + (12\u221a14/5)^2 + E*(12\u221a14/5)=0.\n\nWait, circle equation is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x + Ey=0.\n\nSo, plug C into circle equation:\n\n(22/5)^2 + (12\u221a14/5)^2 -5*(22/5) + E*(12\u221a14/5)=0.\n\nWait, no, let me compute E from the circle equation.\n\nWait, circle equation is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -5x + Ey =0.\n\nSince point C(22/5,12\u221a14/5) lies on the circle:\n\n(22/5)^2 + (12\u221a14/5)^2 -5*(22/5) + E*(12\u221a14/5)=0\n\nCompute each term:\n\n(22/5)^2=484/25\n\n(12\u221a14/5)^2=(144*14)/25=2016/25\n\n-5*(22/5)= -22\n\nE*(12\u221a14/5)= (12\u221a14 E)/5\n\nSo, 484/25 +2016/25 -22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0\n\nCompute 484 +2016=2500, so 2500/25=100\n\nSo, 100 -22 + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0\n\n78 + (12\u221a14 E)/5=0\n\nThus, (12\u221a14 E)/5= -78\n\nSo, 12\u221a14 E= -390\n\nThus, E= -390/(12\u221a14)= -65/(2\u221a14)= -65\u221a14/(28)\n\nThus, E= -65\u221a14/28.\n\nSo, going back to the tangent equation at C:\n\n19x + (24\u221a14 +5E)y + (-110 +12\u221a14 E)=0.\n\nPlugging E= -65\u221a14/28:\n\nCompute each term:\n\n24\u221a14 +5E=24\u221a14 +5*(-65\u221a14/28)=24\u221a14 - (325\u221a14)/28\n\nConvert 24\u221a14 to 28 denominator: 24\u221a14= 672\u221a14/28\n\nSo, 672\u221a14/28 -325\u221a14/28= (672 -325)\u221a14/28=347\u221a14/28\n\nSimilarly, -110 +12\u221a14 E= -110 +12\u221a14*(-65\u221a14/28)= -110 - (12*65*14)/28\n\nCompute 12*65*14= 10920\n\nDivide by28: 10920/28=390\n\nThus, -110 -390= -500\n\nThus, equation becomes:\n\n19x + (347\u221a14/28)y -500=0\n\nSo, equation of tangent at C is 19x + (347\u221a14/28)y =500.\n\nWait, let me double-check:\n\nWait, 24\u221a14 +5E=24\u221a14 +5*(-65\u221a14/28)=24\u221a14 -325\u221a14/28.\n\n24\u221a14= (24*28\u221a14)/28=672\u221a14/28\n\nSo, 672\u221a14/28 -325\u221a14/28=347\u221a14/28.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSimilarly, -110 +12\u221a14 E= -110 +12\u221a14*(-65\u221a14)/28= -110 - (12*65*14)/28.\n\n12*65=780, 780/28=390/7, but wait:\n\nWait, 12\u221a14*E=12\u221a14*(-65\u221a14)/28=12*(-65)*(\u221a14)^2 /28=12*(-65)*14 /28.\n\nCompute 12*65=780, 780*14=10920.\n\n10920/28=10920 divided by 28: 28*390=10920.\n\nSo, 10920/28=390.\n\nThus, 12\u221a14 E= -390, so -110 -390= -500.\n\nThus, the tangent at C is 19x + (347\u221a14/28)y -500=0.\n\nSo, equation is 19x + (347\u221a14/28)y=500.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at B is 5x + Ey=25, from earlier.\n\nBut we have E= -65\u221a14/28, so tangent at B is 5x + (-65\u221a14/28)y=25.\n\nSo, equation is 5x - (65\u221a14/28)y=25.\n\nSo, now, point D is the intersection of tangent at B and tangent at C.\n\nSo, solve the system:\n\n1) 5x - (65\u221a14/28)y=25\n\n2) 19x + (347\u221a14/28)y=500.\n\nLet me write them as:\n\nEquation 1: 5x - (65\u221a14/28)y=25\n\nEquation 2:19x + (347\u221a14/28)y=500\n\nLet me denote (65\u221a14/28)=A, and (347\u221a14/28)=B.\n\nSo, equation 1:5x - A y=25\n\nEquation 2:19x + B y=500.\n\nSo, equations:\n\n5x - A y=25\n\n19x + By=500.\n\nWe can write equation 1 as 5x - Ay=25.\n\nEquation 2:19x + By=500.\n\nLet me write equations:\n\n5x - A y=25\n\n19x + By=500.\n\nLet me solve for x and y.\n\nMultiply equation 1 and 2.\n\nMultiply equation 1 by 19: 95x - (65\u221a14/28)y=25.\n\nEquation 1: 5x - (65\u221a14/28)y=25.\n\nEquation 2:19x + (347\u221a14/28)y=500.\n\nLet me write it as:\n\n5x - (65\u221a14/28)y=25 ...(1)\n\n19x + (347\u221a14/28)y=500 ...(2)\n\nLet me solve equations (1) and (2) simultaneously.\n\nLet me denote k=\u221a14/28. So, k=\u221a14/28.\n\nThen equation (1):5x - (65k)y=25.\n\nEquation (2):19x + (347k)y=500.\n\nSo, we have:\n\n5x -65k y=25 ...(1)\n\n19x +347k y=500 ...(2)\n\nLet me solve for x and y.\n\nLet me multiply equation (1) by 19 and equation (2) by5 to eliminate x.\n\nEquation (1)*19: 95x - 1235k y=475 ...(1a)\n\nEquation (2)*5: 95x + 1735k y=2500 ...(2a)\n\nNow subtract (1a) from (2a):\n\n(95x +1735k y) - (95x -1235k y)=2500 -475\n\nWhich is:\n\n(95x -95x) + (1735k y +1235k y)=2025\n\nSo, 3470k y=2025.\n\nThus, y=2025/(3470k).\n\nBut k=\u221a14/28, so y=2025/(3470*(\u221a14/28))=2025*28/(3470\u221a14)\n\nSimplify 2025 and 3470.\n\nLet me compute GCD of 2025 and 3470.\n\n2025: factors 5^3 * 3^4.\n\n3470: 3470=2*5*347. Hmm, 3470 \u00f7 2025: 3470=2025*1 + 3470-2025=1445.\n\nThen GCD(2025,3470). Let's compute GCD(2025,3470.\n\nWait, 3470 divided by 5 is 694. 2025 is 45^2, 3470 is 2*5*3470.\n\nWait, perhaps better to reduce the fraction 2025/3470.\n\nLet me compute 2025 \u00f75=405, 3470 \u00f7 5=694. So, 3470=5*694. 2025=45^2=9*225=9*9*25=5^2*3^4. Maybe GCD(2025,3470)=5.\n\nSo, 2025=5*405, 3470=5*694.\n\nSo, 2025/3470=405/694.\n\nSo, y= (405/694)/\u221a14.\n\nWait, but in terms of y:\n\ny= (2025*28)/(3470\u221a14)= (2025*28)/(3470\u221a14).\n\nBut 2025=45^2, 28=4*7, 3470=5*694, 14=2*7.\n\nSimplify:\n\n(45^2 *4*7)/(5*694*\u221a14).\n\nHmm, complicated.\n\nAlternatively, compute y=2025/(3470k)=2025/(3470*(\u221a14/28))=2025*28/(3470\u221a14).\n\nSo, y= (2025*28)/(3470\u221a14).\n\nLet me compute 2025*28=56,700\n\n3470\u221a14\u22483470*3.7417\u224812,964. So, 56,700 /12,964\u22484.36.\n\nBut perhaps better to keep it symbolic.\n\nCompute y= (56,700)/(3470\u221a14)= (56,700/3470)/\u221a14= (56,700 \u00f73470= 16.25). Wait, 3470*16=55,520, 55,520+3470=58,990, which is more than 56,700, so perhaps 16.25 is incorrect.\n\nWait, 3470*16=55,520. 56,700-55,520=1,180. 1,180/3470\u22480.34. So, total\u224816.34.\n\nBut anyway, let's keep it as y=56,700/(3470\u221a14)= (56,700/3470)/\u221a14= (16.25)/\u221a14.\n\nWait, 56,700 \u00f73470=16.25? Let me check:\n\n3470*16=55,520.\n\n56,700-55,520=1,180.\n\n1,180/3470=0.34.\n\nSo, total is 16.34. So, 16.34/\u221a14\u224816.34/3.7417\u22484.36.\n\nBut perhaps exact value is not needed yet.\n\nWait, maybe I can rationalize y.\n\ny= (2025*28)/(3470\u221a14)= (56,700)/(3470\u221a14).\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by \u221a14:\n\ny= (56,700\u221a14)/(3470*14)= (56,700\u221a14)/48,580.\n\nSimplify 56,700/48,580: divide numerator and denominator by 10: 5,670/4,858.\n\nCompute GCD(5,670,4,858).\n\n4,858 divides into 5,670 once with remainder 812.\n\n5,670 \u00f74,858=1, remainder 812.\n\n4,858 \u00f7812=5, remainder 4,858-812*5=4,858-4,060=798.\n\n812 \u00f7798=1, remainder 14.\n\n798 \u00f714=57, remainder 0.\n\nSo, GCD is14.\n\nThus, 5,670 \u00f714=405, 4,858 \u00f714=347.\n\nThus, y=405\u221a14 /347.\n\nSo, y=405\u221a14 /347.\n\nSo, y= (405\u221a14)/347.\n\nWait, because:\n\ny= (56,700\u221a14)/48,580= (5,670\u221a14)/48,580= (405\u221a14)/3,470.\n\nWait, no, after simplifying:\n\ny= (5,670\u221a14)/48,580= (5,670\u221a14)/48,580= (5,670/14\u221a14)/347= (405\u221a14)/14.\n\nWait, getting confused.\n\nWait, let's recompute:\n\ny=56,700\u221a14 /48,580= (56,700/48,580)*\u221a14= (1.1698)*\u221a14\u22481.1698*3.7417\u