t A is at (1/3, 4\u221a14/3).\n\nSo, coordinates:\n\nA: (1/3, 4\u221a14/3)\n\nB: (0,0)\n\nC: (9,0)\n\nNow, find point D, which is the intersection of tangents at B and C to the circumcircle.\n\nFirst, find the equation of tangent at B.\n\nEquation of tangent at B: Since B is on the circle, the tangent at B is perpendicular to the radius OB, where O is the circumcircle center.\n\nCompute circumcircle center.\n\nGiven points A(1/3,4\u221a14/3), B(0,0), C(9,0).\n\nFind circumcircle center (h,k).\n\nSolve perpendicular bisectors.\n\nMidpoint of AB: ((1/3 +0)/2, (4\u221a14/3 +0)/2)=(1/6, 2\u221a14/3)\n\nSlope of AB: (4\u221a14/3 -0)/(1/3 -0)= (4\u221a14/3)/(1/3)=4\u221a14.\n\nThus, slope of AB is 4\u221a14, so slope of perpendicular bisector is -1/(4\u221a14).\n\nEquation of perpendicular bisector of AB: passes through midpoint (1/6, 2\u221a14/3) and has slope -1/(4\u221a14).\n\nEquation: y - 2\u221a14/3 = (-1)/(4\u221a14)(x -1/6)\n\nSimilarly, find perpendicular bisector of BC.\n\nMidpoint of BC: ((0 +9)/2, (0 +0)/2)=(9/2, 0)\n\nSlope of BC: (0 -0)/(9 -0)=0, so it's horizontal. Thus, perpendicular bisector is vertical, x=9/2.\n\nSo, equation of perpendicular bisector of BC: x=9/2.\n\nNow, find intersection point of two perpendicular bisectors: x=9/2, and the other one.\n\nFrom perpendicular bisector of AB:\n\ny - 2\u221a14/3 = (-1)/(4\u221a14)(x -1/6)\n\nPlug x=9/2 into this:\n\ny - 2\u221a14/3 = (-1)/(4\u221a14)(9/2 -1/6)= (-1)/(4\u221a14)( (27/6 -1/6) )= (-1)/(4\u221a14)(26/6)= (-1)/(4\u221a14)(13/3)= (-13)/(12\u221a14)\n\nThus, y=2\u221a14/3 -13/(12\u221a14)\n\nRationalize denominator:\n\n2\u221a14/3 = (2\u221a14)/3, and 13/(12\u221a14)=13\u221a14/(12*14)=13\u221a14/168\n\nSo, 2\u221a14/3= (112\u221a14)/168\n\nThus, y=112\u221a14/168 -13\u221a14/168=99\u221a14/168=33\u221a14/56.\n\nThus, center O is at (9/2, 33\u221a14/56).\n\nCompute radius squared: distance from O to B squared.\n\nPoint B is at (0,0), O is at (9/2,33\u221a14/56).\n\nCompute distance squared from O to B:\n\nO(9/2, 33\u221a14/56), radius squared:\n\n(9/2 -0)^2 + (33\u221a14/56 -0)^2 + (33\u221a14/56 -0)^2.\n\nWait, no, compute distance squared from O to B.\n\nPoint B is at (0,0), so distance squared is h^2 +k= (9/2)^2 + (33\u221a14/56)^2 - (O to B)^2.\n\nWait, better to compute distance from O to B.\n\nPoint O is (9/2, 33\u221a14/56). Wait, no, wait, no, O is (9/2, 33\u221a14/56). Wait, no, wait, no, the y-coordinate was 2\u221a14/3 -13/(12\u221a14). Wait, wait, perhaps I made a mistake earlier.\n\nWait, let's recast the y-coordinate.\n\nWait, earlier, I had:\n\ny - 2\u221a14/3 = (-1)/(4\u221a14)(x -1/6)\n\nSo, when x=9/2,\n\ny= 2\u221a14/3 + (-1)/(4\u221a14)(9/2 -1/6)=2\u221a14/3 + (-1)/(4\u221a14)(13/6)\n\nCompute (-1)/(4\u221a14)*(13/6)= (-13)/(24\u221a14)\n\nSo, y=2\u221a14/3 -13/(24\u221a14)\n\nConvert 2\u221a14/3 to 16\u221a14/24,\n\nSo, y=16\u221a14/24 -13\u221a14/24=3\u221a14/24=\u221a14/8.\n\nThus, center O is at (9/2, \u221a14/24? Wait, no:\n\nWait, 2\u221a14/3 is equal to (2\u221a14)/3, which is approximately 2.66, wait, no, no, let me see.\n\nWait, 2\u221a14/3 is approximately 3.5\u221a14, and 13/(24\u221a14.\n\nWait, perhaps better to rationalize.\n\nWait, 2\u221a14/3 -13/(24\u221a14). Let me rationalize both terms:\n\n2\u221a14/3= (2\u221a14)/3= (8\u221a14)/something.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to keep as is.\n\nSo, center is at (9/2, 2\u221a14/3 -13/(12\u221a14)).\n\nWait, this is getting too messy. Maybe I made a mistake earlier.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use coordinates to find point D, but this might be too computation-heavy.\n\nWait, maybe instead, use the property that in a circle, the polar of D is the line BC.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since AD intersects the circle at P, then AP is the power of A with respect to the circle.\n\nWait, but A is on the circle, so power of A is zero. Hmm, that doesn't help.\n\nWait, but D is the ex-tangent point. So, power of D with respect to circle is equal to DB^2=DC^2.\n\nPower of 