rom A(1/3,4\u221a14/3):\n\nx=1/3, y=4\u221a14/3.\n\nSo, equation:\n\n225/9 + (-9/3) + (8\u221a14 f)/3=0.\n\nWait, no, let me compute again:\n\nFrom A: 1/9 + (16*14)/9 + 2g*(1/3) + 2f*(4\u221a14/3)=0.\n\nWhich is 1/9 +224/9 + 2*(-9/2)*(1/3) + 2f*(4\u221a14/3)=0.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n1/9 +224/9=225/9=25.\n\n2*(-9/2)*(1/3)= (-9)*(1/3)= -3.\n\nSo, 25 -3 + (8\u221a14/3)f=0.\n\n22 + (8\u221a14/3)f=0.\n\nThus, (8\u221a14/3)f= -22.\n\nSo, f= -22*(3)/(8\u221a14)= -66/(8\u221a14)= -33/(4\u221a14).\n\nRationalize denominator: f= -33\u221a14/(4*14)= -33\u221a14/56.\n\nThus, equation of circle is x^2 + y^2 +2*(-9/2)x +2*(-33\u221a14/56)y=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\nx^2 + y^2 -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nSo, circle equation is x^2 + y^2 -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nNow, to find equations of tangents at B and C.\n\nTangent at B(0,0):\n\nFor a circle with center (h,k), tangent at (x1,y1) is (x1 - h)(x - h) + (y1 - k)(y - k)=r^2.\n\nWait, no, the equation of tangent at (x1,y1) on circle (x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2 is (x1 - h)(x - h) + (y1 - k)(y - k)=r^2.\n\nBut here, circle is x^2 + y^2 -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nComplete squares:\n\nx^2 -9x + y^2 - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nx^2 -9x= (x - 9/2)^2 -81/4.\n\ny^2 - (33\u221a14/28)y= (y - 33\u221a14/56)^2 - (33\u221a14/56)^2.\n\nSo, circle equation becomes:\n\n(x - 9/2)^2 -81/4 + (y - 33\u221a14/56)^2 - (33\u221a14/56)^2=0.\n\nThus, center is (9/2, 33\u221a14/56), radius squared is 81/4 + (33\u221a14/56)^2.\n\nSo, equation of tangent at B(0,0):\n\n(0 -9/2)(x -9/2) + (0 -33\u221a14/56)(y -33\u221a14/56)= (81/4) + (33\u221a14/56)^2.\n\nSimplify:\n\n-9/2*(x -9/2) -33\u221a14/56*(y -33\u221a14/56)=81/4 + (33\u221a14/56)^2.\n\nBut since B is on the circle, the tangent at B is given by the equation:\n\nUsing point form: For circle x^2 + y^2 +2gx +2fy=0, tangent at (x1,y1) is xx1 + yy1 + g(x +x1) + f(y + y1)=0.\n\nSo, for circle x^2 + y^2 -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0, tangent at B(0,0) is:\n\n0*x +0*y -9*(x +0) - (33\u221a14/28)*(y +0)=0.\n\nSimplify: -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at C(9,0):\n\nUsing same formula, tangent at C(9,0) is:\n\nx*9 + y*0 -9*(x +9) - (33\u221a14/28)*(y +0)=0.\n\nSimplify: 9x -9x -81 - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nSo, -81 - (33\u221a14/28)y=0 => y= -28/33\u221a14.\n\nSo, equation of tangent at C is y= -28/(33\u221a14).\n\nSimilarly, tangent at B is -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nSimplify tangent at B: -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0 => 9x + (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nMultiply both sides by 28: 252x +33\u221a14 y=0.\n\nSo, equation is 252x +33\u221a14 y=0.\n\nSo, equations:\n\nTangent at B: 252x +33\u221a14 y=0.\n\nTangent at C: y= -28/(33\u221a14).\n\nFind point D, the intersection of these two.\n\nFrom tangent at C: y= -28/(33\u221a14).\n\nPlug into tangent at B:\n\n252x +33\u221a14*(-28/(33\u221a14))=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n252x - (33\u221a14)*(28)/(33\u221a14)=0.\n\nSimplify: 252x -28=0 => 252x=28 => x= (28)/(252)=1/9.\n\nThus, point D is (252x +33\u221a14 y)=0.\n\nWait, but we have y= -28/(33\u221a14).\n\nThus, D is (x, y= -28/(33\u221a14).\n\nCompute x-coordinate:\n\nFrom tangent at B: 252x +33\u221a14 y=0.\n\nSo, substituting y= -28/(33\u221a14) into tangent at B.\n\nWait, no, better to compute:\n\nFrom tangent at C: y= -28/(33\u221a14).\n\nWait, let me compute x:\n\n252x +33\u221a14*( -28/(33\u221a14) )=0\n\n252x -28=0 => 252x=28 => x=28/252=1/9.\n\nThus, D is at (1/9, -28/(33\u221a14)).\n\nSimplify coordinates:\n\nx=1/9, y= -28/(33\u221a14)= -28\u221a14/(33*14)= -2\u221a14/(33).\n\nThus, D is (1/9, -2\u221a14/33).\n\nSo, coordinates of D: (1/9, -2\u221a14/33).\n\nNow, we need to find point P where AD intersects the circle again.\n\nEquation of AD: from A(1/9, 4\u221a14/3) to D(1/9, -2\u221a14/33).\n\nWait, both points have x=1/9, so line AD is vertical line x=1/9.\n\nThus, line AD is x=1/9.\n\nThus, intersection of x=1/9 with circle is point A and point P.\n\nThus, to find P, solve for y when x=1/9 in the circle equation.\n\nCircle equation: x^2 + y^2 -9x - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nPlug x=1/9:\n\n(1/81) + y^2 -9*(1/9) - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n1/81 + y^2 -1 - (33\u221a14/28)y=0.\n\nSo, y^2 - (33\u221a14/28)y + (1/81 -1)=0.\n\nCompute constants:\n\n1/81 -1= (1 -81)/81= -80/81.\n\nThus, equation: y^2 - (33\u221a14/28)y -80/81=0.\n\nQuadratic in y: y^2 - (33\u221a14/28)y -80/81=0.\n\nSolve for y:\n\nUsing quadratic formula:\n\ny= [ (33\u221a14/28) \u00b1 sqrt( (33\u221a14/28)^2 +4*1*(80/81) ) ] /2.\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n(33\u221a14/28)^2 + 320/81.\n\nCompute first term:\n\n(33^2 *14)/(28^2)= (1089 *14)/(784)= (15246)/(784).\n\nSimplify 15246/784: divide numerator and denominator by 14: 15246/14=1093.571, wait, maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, 15246 \u00f714=1093.571, but 784 \u00f714=56. So, 15246/784=1093.571/56.\n\nWait, perhaps better to keep as is.\n\nCompute 15246/784 + 320/81.\n\nConvert to common denominator.\n\nCompute 15246/784= (15246*81)/(784*81)= compute 15246*81: 15246*80=1,217,680; 15246*1=15,246. Total=1,232,926.\n\nSimilarly, 320/81= (320*784)/(81*784)= 320*784=250,880.\n\nThus, discriminant=1,232,926/784 +250,880/784= (1,232,926 +250,880)/784=1,483,806/784.\n\nSimplify 1,483,806 \u00f74=370,951.5, which is not integer. Maybe it's better to compute sqrt(1,483,806)/28.\n\nWait, discriminant= sqrt( (33\u221a14/28)^2 +320/81 ).\n\nWait, perhaps compute numerically:\n\nCompute (33\u221a14/28)^2:\n\n33^2=1089, (\u221a14)^2=14, so 1089*14=15246.\n\n15246/(28^2)=15246/784\u224819.43.\n\nCompute 4*1*(80/81)=320/81\u22483.9506.\n\nThus, discriminant\u224819.43 +3.9506\u224823.3806.\n\nSo, sqrt(23.3806)\u22484.835.\n\nThus, y\u2248[4.835 \u00b14.835]/2.\n\nWait, wait, no. Wait, quadratic formula is [b \u00b1 sqrt(D)]/(2a).\n\nHere, a=1, b= -33\u221a14/28\u2248-33*3.7417/28\u2248-33*0.133\u2248-4.389.\n\nWait, actually, discriminant is sqrt( (33\u221a14/28)^2 +320/81 )\u2248sqrt(19.43 +3.95)=sqrt(23.38)\u22484.835.\n\nThus, y= [ (33\u221a14/28) \u00b14.835 ] /2.\n\nCompute:\n\n33\u221a14\u224833*3.7417\u2248123.6.\n\nThus, 33\u221a14/28\u2248123.6/28\u22484.414.\n\nThus, y\u2248(4.414 \u00b14.835)/2.\n\nSo, two solutions:\n\n1. (4.414 +4.835)/2\u22489.249/2\u22484.624.\n\n2. (4.414 -4.835)/2\u2248-0.421/2\u2248-0.210.\n\nSo, y\u22484.624 and y\u2248-0.210.\n\nBut point A is at y=4\u221a14/3\u22484*3.7417/3\u22484.983.\n\nWait, but line AD is x=1/9, so when x=1/9, y=4\u221a14/3\u22484.983 and y=-2\u221a14/33\u2248-0.210.\n\nWait, so the circle intersects AD at A and P. So, since A is already on the circle, the other intersection is P.\n\nThus, P is the other point, y\u2248-0.210, which is P.\n\nThus, point P is at (1/9, -2\u221a14/33).\n\nWait, but that is D.\n\nWait, no. Wait, D is at (1/9, -2\u221a14/33), which is D.\n\nWait, wait, no. Wait, point D is at (1/9, -2\u221a14/33\u2248-0.210.\n\nThus, the points are A(1/9, 4\u221a14/3) and D(1/9, -2\u221a14/33).\n\nWait, so when we plug x=1/9 into the circle equation, we get two points: A and D?\n\nWait, no. Wait, point D is the external point, not on the circle.\n\nWait, but in the circle equation, point D is not on the circle because it's the ex-tangent.\n\nWait, so the line AD intersects the circle at A and P.\n\nThus, when solving for y when x=1/9, we get two points: A and P.\n\nSince A is already on the circle, the other intersection is P.\n\nThus, in our quadratic, one solution is y=4\u221a14/3, which is A, and the other is y\u2248-0.210, which is P.\n\nThus, we need to compute y-coordinate of P, which is -2\u221a14/33.\n\nWait, let me compute that.\n\nFrom the quadratic equation:\n\ny = [ (33\u221a14/28) \u00b1 sqrt( (33\u221a14/28)^2 + 320/81 ) ] / 2.\n\nWait, but if we already know that one solution is 4\u221a14/3, which is y\u22484.983, and the other is y\u2248-0.210.\n\nThus, AP is the distance from A(1/9,4\u221a14/3) to P(1/9, -2\u221a14/33).\n\nCompute distance AP:\n\nSince x-coordinates are same, it's just difference in y-coordinates.\n\nSo, AP= |4\u221a14/3 - (-2\u221a14/33)|= |4\u221a14/3 +2\u221a14/33|.\n\nConvert to common