press c in terms of b?\n\nFrom the earlier equation: 3(4 sqrt(30) + b + c)= (b c sqrt(30))/13\n\nMultiply both sides by13:\n\n39 sqrt(30) +39 b +39 c= b c sqrt(30)\n\nWhich is the same as previous.\n\nHmm, maybe I need another approach.\n\nWait, we have a=4 sqrt(30), angle A with cos A=7/13.\n\nUsing the formula for inradius:\n\nr= (Area)/(s)\n\nGiven r=6, so 6= (Area)/(s)\n\nWe already have Area= (b c sin A)/2= (b c sqrt(30))/13\n\nSo, 6= [ (b c sqrt(30))/13 ] / s\n\nWhich is 6= (b c sqrt(30))/(13 s)\n\nSo, s= (a +b +c)/2= (4 sqrt(30) +b +c)/2\n\nSo, 6= (b c sqrt(30))/(13*( (4 sqrt(30) +b +c)/2 ))\n\nSimplify denominator:\n\n13*( (4 sqrt(30) +b +c)/2 )= (13/2)(4 sqrt(30) +b +c)\n\nSo, 6= (b c sqrt(30)) / [ (13/2)(4 sqrt(30) +b +c) ]\n\nMultiply both sides by denominator:\n\n6*(13/2)(4 sqrt(30) +b +c)=b c sqrt(30)\n\nSimplify 6*(13/2)= (6*13/2)(4 sqrt(30) +b +b +c)\n\nSimplify:\n\n6*(13/2)=39\n\nSo, 6*(13/2)(4 sqrt(30) +b +b +c)=6*(13/2)(4 sqrt(30) +b +c)\n\nWait, perhaps I made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, this seems similar to what I had earlier.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the formula that relates r, R, and the distance between inradius and circumradius.\n\nWait, perhaps using the formula that relates inradius, circumradius, and other parameters.\n\nBut I don't recall a direct formula.\n\nAlternatively, since we know that O is the circumcenter and I is the inradius, and IA is perpendicular to OI.\n\nHmm, maybe we can use vector methods or coordinate geometry.\n\nLet me try coordinate geometry.\n\nLet\u2019s place point O at the origin (0,0). Then, since O is the circumcenter, the coordinates of A, B, C lie on the circumcircle with radius 13.\n\nLet\u2019s denote coordinates:\n\nLet\u2019s suppose that I is the incenter. Let\u2019s denote vector IA is perpendicular to vector OI.\n\nGiven that IA is perpendicular to OI, their dot product is zero.\n\nSo, if we can express vectors IA and OI in coordinates, their dot product is zero.\n\nBut to do this, we need coordinat