e expressed in terms of the triangle's sides.\n\nBut this seems getting complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use properties of triangle centers.\n\nGiven that IA is perpendicular to OI, so triangle AIO is right-angled at I.\n\nSo, in triangle AIO, we have right angle at I.\n\nSo, by Pythagoras: OA\u00b2 = AI\u00b2 + IO\u00b2\n\nGiven OA is the circumradius, 13.\n\nSo, 13\u00b2 = AI\u00b2 + IO\u00b2.\n\nSo, AI\u00b2 + IO\u00b2 = 169.\n\nWe can compute AI and IO.\n\nWe know that IO is the distance between incenter and circumradius.\n\nThere is a formula for IO: IO\u00b2 = R(R - 2r), where R is circumradius and r is inradius.\n\nWait, is that correct?\n\nWait, I recall that in a triangle, the distance between incenter and circumradius is given by IO\u00b2 = R\u00b2 - 2Rr.\n\nWait, let me check.\n\nYes, the formula is IO\u00b2 = R\u00b2 - 2Rr.\n\nSo, IO\u00b2 = 13\u00b2 - 2*13*6= 169 - 156=13.\n\nThus, IO= sqrt(13).\n\nSo, from the Pythagoras: AI\u00b2 + IO\u00b2=169 => AI\u00b2 +13=169 => AI\u00b2=156 => AI=sqrt(156)=2*sqrt(39).\n\nSo, AI=2*sqrt(39).\n\nAlso, AI is the distance from incenter to vertex A.\n\nThere is a formula for the distance from inradius to vertex A: AI= r / sin(A/2), where r is inradius.\n\nWait, is that correct?\n\nYes, in a triangle, the distance from the inradius to vertex A is AI = r / sin(A/2).\n\nSo, AI=6 / sin(A/2)=2 sqrt(39).\n\nTherefore, sin(A/2)=6 / (2 sqrt(39))=3 / sqrt(39)=sqrt(39)/13.\n\nWait, let me compute sin(A/2)=3 / sqrt(39).\n\nSimplify: sqrt(39)=sqrt(13*3), so sin(A/2)=3 / sqrt(13*3)=sqrt(3/13).\n\nWait, sin(A/2)=sqrt(3/13).\n\nSo, A/2= arcsin(sqrt(3/13)).\n\nTherefore, A=2 arcsin(sqrt(3/13)).\n\nAlternatively, cos(A)=1 - 2 sin\u00b2(A/2)=1 - 2*(3/13)=1 - 6/13=7/13.\n\nThus, cos A=7/13.\n\nSo, angle A has cosine 7/13.\n\nTherefore, from the Law of Cosines in triangle ABC:\n\na\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2bc cos A.\n\nBut since a is BC, opposite angle A.\n\nBut I don't know sides b and c.\n\nWait, but we might need to find AB * AC, which is c * b.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula for area.\n\nArea= r*s=6s.\n\nAlso, area= (1/2)ab sin C.\n\nWait, but without knowing angles or sides, maybe use the formula:\n\nIn any triangle, a*b*c=4R*area.\n\nSo, area= (a*b*c)/(4R)= (a*b*c)/52.\n\nBut we also have area=6s.\n\nSo, 6s=(a*b*c)/52 => s=(a*b*c)/(52*6)= (a*b*c)/312.\n\nBut s=(a + b + c)/2.\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula:\n\nIn a triangle, AI = r / sin(A/2), which we have as 2 sqrt(39).\n\nAlso, the formula for exradius opposite to A is R_a= (bc)/(s - a).\n\nBut I don't know if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula for the length of angle bisector, but not sure.\n\nWait, another approach: in triangle ABC, AI is the distance from inradius to vertex A, which is 2 sqrt(39).\n\nWe have AI=2 sqrt(39), and we have cos A=7/13.\n\nAlso, in any triangle, the distance from inradius to vertex A is given by AI= r / sin(A/2)=6 / sin(A/2)=2 sqrt(39).\n\nSo, sin(A/2)=3 / sqrt(39)=sqrt(3/13).\n\nTherefore, cos(A/2)=sqrt(1 - 3/13)=sqrt(10/13).\n\nThen, using the half-angle formula:\n\ntan(A/2)=sin(A/2)/cos(A/2)=sqrt(3/13)/sqrt(10/13)=sqrt(3/10).\n\nSo, tan(A/2)=sqrt(3/10).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula for the semiperimeter.\n\nWait, another idea: using the formula for the length of the inradius:\n\nr = (Area)/s.\n\nWe have r=6, so Area=6s.\n\nAlso, Area can be expressed as (1/2)ab sin C.\n\nBut without knowing the sides, it's tricky.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula that relates AI, angle A, and other elements.\n\nIn triangle ABC, AI= (r)/ sin(A/2)=2 sqrt(39).\n\nSo, 2 sqrt(39)=6 / sin(A/2) => sin(A/2)=6 / (2 sqrt(39))=3 / sqrt(39)=sqrt(3/13).\n\nSo, sin(A/2)=sqrt(3/13), as above.\n\nThus, cos(A/2)=sqrt(10/13).\n\nThen, tan(A/2)=sqrt(3/10).\n\nAlso, in triangle ABC, the length of the tangent from A to the inradius is given by:\n\ntan(A/2)= (r)/(s - a).\n\nWait, yes, the formula is tan(A/2)= r / (s - a).\n\nSo, tan(A/2)=6 / (s - a)=sqrt(3/10